---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/potassium`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Potassium Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Potassium', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Potassium: Blood Pressure, DASH Diet, and Salt Substitutes"
  description="Potassium reference — 4,700 mg daily target, DASH diet effect on blood pressure, KCl salt substitutes, banana myth, and the 5 mmol/L hyperkalaemia ceiling."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/potassium", label: "Potassium" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Potassium</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Potassium is the main intracellular cation. It maintains membrane potential, drives
      the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump that keeps cells alive, and is required for muscle contraction
      and cardiac rhythm. Chronic intake shortfalls are one of the largest population-scale
      nutrient problems in the developed world; acute derangements are life-threatening.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adult men (IOM AI):</strong> 3,400 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Adult women (IOM AI):</strong> 2,600 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>DASH-diet target for blood pressure:</strong> ~4,700 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Actual US intake (median):</strong> ~2,300 mg/day — well below the AI for both sexes.</li>
      <li><strong>No IOM upper limit for healthy adults</strong> — but supplements over 99 mg per dose require prescription in the US because of hyperkalaemia risk.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Blood pressure — the DASH evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The DASH-Sodium trial demonstrated that raising dietary potassium (via fruits,
      vegetables, and low-fat dairy) while cutting sodium reduces systolic BP by 8–14 mm Hg
      in hypertensive adults — larger than most single antihypertensive drugs. The 2021
      Chinese SSaSS trial showed KCl-based salt substitute (75% NaCl / 25% KCl) reduced
      stroke, cardiovascular events, and death in 21,000 rural adults over 5 years.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The banana myth</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A medium banana has 422 mg potassium — respectable, but not exceptional. A baked
      potato with skin has 926 mg. A cup of white beans has 1,189 mg. Bananas are famous
      for potassium mostly because they were the food doctors could dependably recommend
      to K-deficient hospital patients decades ago; they're not the densest source.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Hyperkalaemia — when potassium becomes dangerous</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Serum potassium above 5.5 mmol/L causes peaked T waves on ECG; above 6.5 mmol/L causes
      broadened QRS and risk of ventricular fibrillation. Groups who should NOT freely
      increase potassium intake or use salt substitutes:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Chronic kidney disease stage 3B or worse (eGFR &lt; 45)</li>
      <li>Anyone on an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan)</li>
      <li>Anyone on spironolactone, eplerenone, amiloride, or triamterene</li>
      <li>Type 4 renal tubular acidosis, Addison's disease, hypoaldosteronism</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">High-potassium foods</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Baked potato with skin (1 medium): 926 mg</li>
      <li>White beans (1 cup cooked): 1,189 mg</li>
      <li>Lentils (1 cup cooked): 731 mg</li>
      <li>Cooked spinach (1 cup): 839 mg</li>
      <li>Sweet potato (1 medium baked): 542 mg</li>
      <li>Salmon (3 oz): 534 mg</li>
      <li>Avocado (½ fruit): 487 mg</li>
      <li>Banana (1 medium): 422 mg</li>
      <li>Yoghurt (1 cup plain): 380 mg</li>
      <li>Coconut water (1 cup): 600 mg</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Never take OTC potassium supplements or use
      salt substitutes if you have kidney disease or take K-sparing medications without
      checking with your prescriber. Sudden weakness and palpitations after a potassium load
      is a medical emergency.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/potassium" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
